{
  name: "axi_agent"
  testpoints: [
    {
      name: smoke
      desc: '''Random single transfers and bursts from the manager agent to the subordinate agent,
            which answers from the defaults of its configuration: INCR, WRAP and FIXED bursts,
            narrow and unaligned transfers, INCR bursts that end on a 4KB boundary, and INCR bursts
            of more than 16 beats, with a write and a read of 256 (H.c A3.4.1). Before them, the
            agent's transfer sequences, randomised with only the bus properties, send bursts whose
            every byte must have an address that the bus carries and whose USER fits an ARUSER one
            bit narrower than AWUSER, and a single write and a write burst are each started three
            times and read back, the burst's last start with only its data cleared, so that each
            start must send new data and report only its own request and responses. A listed W
            data sequence started twice must write its second data. The scoreboard compares what
            each transfer sequence got back with what the manager's monitor saw and with what the
            subordinate's monitor saw, checks beat counts, response codes and read data against a
            reference memory, and compares the subordinate's memory with it at the end. Every W
            burst on every bus must belong to an AW request and end with WLAST by the end of the
            test (H.c A3.3).'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_smoke"]
    }
    {
      name: widths
      desc: '''The smoke traffic on other buses: 128 and 1024 bit data, 32 bit data with 2 bit IDs,
            and a 12 bit address. On a narrow bus, only the ID, address and WSTRB bits that it has
            connect the two agents. The other bits are undriven, and every check must ignore
            them.'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_wide_bus", "axi_agent_widest_bus", "axi_agent_narrow_id",
              "axi_agent_narrow_addr", "axi_agent_narrow_pins"]
    }
    {
      name: top_addr
      desc: '''Bursts anywhere in a 64 bit address space from the agent's transfer sequences,
            randomised with only the bus properties. Then WRAP bursts of several sizes and lengths
            in the last bytes of the space, each beat read back on its own at the address that the
            burst address equations give it (H.c A3.4.1), and a write to the last eight bytes cut
            by a reset.'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_top_addr"]
    }
    {
      name: out_of_order
      desc: '''Many transactions in flight, with the subordinate answering writes and reads out of
            order across IDs and interleaving read data across IDs, while keeping the order within
            each ID (AMBA AXI H.c A5.1). The test needs reordered writes and reads, interleaved
            read beats and requests that reuse an ID in flight.'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_out_of_order"]
    }
    {
      name: backpressure
      desc: '''Random READY on every channel at both ends, with READY before VALID, READY held low
            after VALID and back-to-back transfers, and random response latency. The test needs
            each of these on all five channels. Then fixed READY delays stall every transfer the
            same number of edges with the READY-wait bound of the assertions set to it, and the
            longest stall on each channel must reach the bound. Last, with one write and one read
            in flight at most, every AW and AR must wait its full READY delay after VALID, also
            when VALID came before the previous transaction completed.'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_backpressure"]
    }
    {
      name: idle_x
      desc: '''Both agents drive X on every payload whose VALID is low, so a receiver that samples
            outside a handshake picks up X.'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_x_idle"]
    }
    {
      name: errors
      desc: '''SLVERR and DECERR, from an address region and from the subordinate's response
            sequence, each predicted by the scoreboard from the policy that the subordinate gave
            the transaction. A write answered with an error leaves memory unchanged.'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_errors"]
    }
    {
      name: reset
      desc: '''Resets in the middle of traffic, at least one of which cuts an accepted write. Every
            transaction in flight is dropped, VALID is low at every edge in reset and at the first
            edge after it (H.c A3.1.2), and nothing from before a reset is answered after it. Four
            more resets follow. The first cuts a write that a second write to the same bytes waits
            behind, and the second write must be sent after reset release and complete. The next
            cuts the second start of a reused write sequence, which must then report no response and
            an incomplete transaction, and the third cuts the second starts of reused B and R accept
            sequences, which must report no response and take no handshake. A write and a read
            start in the time step where the fourth is released. Both must complete, with VALID low
            at the first edge after the release.'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_reset"]
    }
    {
      name: off_edge
      desc: '''Requests that reach the manager drivers between clock edges while READY is already
            high, and B and R accepts that start between edges while VALID is already high. Each
            transfer still takes exactly one handshake (H.c A3.2.1).'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_off_edge"]
    }
    {
      name: rsp_order
      desc: '''Response policy items go to transactions in the order the subordinate accepts them:
            a read first, a write first, and a write and a read accepted at one edge, where the
            write takes the first item. Transactions after the items run out get the defaults. Then
            single writes, W beat first or AW first, and reads with latencies of 0, 1, 2 and 5
            check that a response with latency L is first valid L + 1 edges after the edge that made
            its transaction answerable.'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_rsp_order"]
    }
    {
      name: mapped_mem
      desc: '''The subordinate backed by a memory model that overrides the byte access methods of
            mem_model: mapped bytes read as the model's pattern until written, writes outside the
            map are dropped and unmapped bytes read as the default byte.'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_mapped_mem"]
    }
    {
      name: lite
      desc: '''AXI4-Lite at 64 and 32 bits, both agents in AXI4-Lite mode, with each strobe policy
            of the subordinate (H.c B1.1). At 32 bits the subordinate sees X on every unstrobed
            WDATA lane, which a manager may drive with any value (A3.4.4).'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_lite", "axi_agent_lite_32"]
    }
    {
      name: exclusive
      desc: '''Exclusive reads and writes, with and without exclusive support in the subordinate,
            including exclusive writes that fail because of an intervening write, also one made
            while the exclusive read waits for its data, no exclusive read, an intervening
            exclusive read of other bytes with the same ID, with or without
            an error response, or an attribute other than AxREGION that differs from the read
            (H.c A7.2), and an exclusive access to the last bytes of a 64 bit address space. The
            subordinate agent orders a read at its AR handshake and a write at the later of its AW
            and last W handshakes. H.c A6.1 allows the other order too. So after a write that was
            in flight when the exclusive read was accepted, and whose data came after it, the next
            two matching exclusive writes must both fail (A7.2.3).'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_exclusive"]
    }
    {
      name: reg_layer
      desc: '''Register writes and checked reads through the register layer, with 32 bit registers
            on 64 and 32 bit buses, AXI4 and AXI4-Lite, many accesses in flight at once and byte
            fields written on their own. On AXI4-Lite every access carries one AxPROT value. A
            two-byte access at an odd address must carry both bytes on their lanes (H.c A3.4.1,
            A3.4.4). One test keeps the mirror with a uvm_reg_predictor fed by the manager's
            monitor, which must ignore SLVERR responses and an exclusive write that fails (H.c
            A7.2.3), and checks that the adapter reports only the bytes an unaligned transfer
            carries. On AXI4, the manager has no ARUSER while AWUSER keeps its width, so register
            reads must fit the AR channel's own USER width.'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_reg_layer", "axi_agent_reg_layer_lite", "axi_agent_reg_layer_lite_32",
              "axi_agent_reg_predictor"]
    }
    {
      name: pulp_interop
      desc: '''The agents run against an independent AXI implementation, pulp_axi. Its random
            manager runs against the subordinate agent, once with INCR bursts of up to 256 beats
            only, so that pulp_axi's own axi_scoreboard checks the read data too. Its AXI4-Lite
            driver runs against the subordinate agent, directly in AXI4-Lite mode and through
            axi_lite_to_axi in AXI4 mode. The manager agent runs against axi_sim_mem,
            axi_rand_slave, axi_lite_regs and, through axi_to_axi_lite, the subordinate agent in
            AXI4-Lite mode. Through axi_to_axi_lite, the subordinate answers some transfers with
            SLVERR, and each write burst must get the first error of its transfers (H.c B1.3.1).
            Write bursts carry beats with no strobes, INCR and FIXED, whose transfers a bridge may
            suppress (B1.1.3).'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_pulp_mgr", "axi_agent_pulp_mgr_incr", "axi_agent_pulp_lite_mgr",
              "axi_agent_sim_mem", "axi_agent_rand_slave", "axi_agent_lite_regs",
              "axi_agent_lite_bridge", "axi_agent_lite_to_axi"]
    }
    {
      name: xbar
      desc: '''Two manager agents against three subordinate agents through pulp_axi's axi_xbar,
            with the transaction limits, latency mode and pipeline of mocha's top-level crossbar,
            64 bit addresses and data, and an address map of its own: routing to every subordinate,
            IDs widened with the index of the manager port, transactions open at several
            subordinates at once, and DECERR from the crossbar for an address that no subordinate
            takes. At the end, each subordinate's memory holds only bytes whose address routes to
            it.'''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["axi_agent_xbar"]
    }
  ]
}
